{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c6261d5fbe10067","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8007\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: E005 lists Investigation RHAISTRAT-8007-E001 in dependencies (a Rule\n10\t    3 rewrite gate per the DAG justification) but gated_by names only E002 — the prototype-alignment\n11\t    gate is unrecorded in gate metadata (E006 records gated_by E001 for the same gate;\n12\t    E005 covers it only in gate_failure_impact prose). Types are correct; per the\n13\t    Criterion 4 rubric this is gating-metadata level 1, not a misclassification.\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n16\t  description: E006 scope item create-action entry point on the list page (opens the\n17\t    instance-creation wizard, routing coordinated with E005) has no corresponding\n18\t    acceptance criterion — add an AC gating the list-page Create action against the\n19\t    wizard route, admin status, and feature flag.\n20\terror: null\n21\t---\n22\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8007\n23\t\n24\t## Review Summary\n25\t\n26\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n27\tRecommendation: accept\n28\t\n29\tcontract validator: OK (`python3 skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8007` exited 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass; only non-mechanical criteria graded below).\n30\t\n31\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition), so the 8-epic multi-team output is expected and is not penalized. The strategy is effort M (3-5 sprints) with a critical path of 4 (E002 → E003 → E005 → E008), within the M-standard heuristic of 3-4.\n32\t\n33\t## Criterion Details\n34\t\n35\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n36\t\n37\tAll 8 HLRs map to at least one epic: the five P0 HLRs (create wizard → E005; list, inspect, delete, real-time status → E006), both P1 HLRs (viewer read-only → E004/E005/E006; air-gapped → E005/E006/E008), and the P2 HLR (inline validation → E005). The traceability matrix in the summary is complete and matches the per-epic HLR Traceability sections.\n38\t\n39\tPriority inheritance: every epic that blocks P0 work (E001, E002, E003 gating E005/E006) is itself P0. E008 is P1 but is a `docs-authoring` epic — exempt per the Jira-priority rule (strategy priority Major → P1); its Rule 11 edges to P0 implementations are not flagged.\n40\t\n41\tPriority collapse: P1 viewer mode, P1 air-gapped, and P2 inline validation are bundled into the P0 epics. This is justified, not collapse: viewer mode is control visibility on the same list/detail surfaces (splitting it would be a thin increment on one surface), air-gapped support is an intrinsic implementation attribute (bundled static assets, no external fetches), and P2 inline validation shares the wizard's form surface and validation logic — building it separately would duplicate the same UI surface. The summary's Health Warnings flag this bundling explicitly with rationale.\n42\t\n43\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n44\t\n45\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: every edge in the summary DAG (E001→E005, E001→E006, E002→E003, E002→E005, E003→E005, E003→E006, E003/E004/E005/E006/E007→E008) has a matching entry in the target epic's `dependencies`, and vice versa. No edge references a nonexistent epic; no cycles. Arrows run dependency → dependent per the `graph TD` convention.\n46\t\n47\tRule 11: the `docs-authoring` epic E008 is blocked by all five main-plan Implementation epics (E003-E007); E001/E002 are Investigations and are correctly exempt.\n48\t\n49\tParallelism: no `konflux-onboarding`/`license-validation`/`repo-onboarding` epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings do not apply. The two deliberate non-edges are correctly justified: E004 (RBAC/flag manifests) → E005/E006 is a same-repo config reference handled as AC gates on the frontend epics (\"operations succeed end-to-end ... with the extended dashboard ClusterRole\"), matching the Rule 13/15 treatment; E007 (passive feast-operator validation) has no code-change artifact for the frontend epics to build against. Neither serializes naturally-parallel work.\n50\t\n51\tMinor (does not reduce score): E006's scope includes a \"create-action entry point on the list page that opens the instance-creation wizard\" whose cross-epic routing coordination with E005 is handled only in prose — no DAG edge (correctly, same repo/team) but also no AC gate on either epic (see Criterion 6).\n52\t\n53\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n54\t\n55\tEach epic is a single component/team tuple: AI Core Dashboard / Dashboard-Frontend Engineering (E001, E004, E005, E006), Feature Store / Feature Store Engineering (E002, E007), UXD / UXD Team (E003), Documentation / Documentation Team (E008). All four component names are canonical per `.context/rhai-components.txt` (validator-confirmed).\n56\t\n57\tNo scope duplication: E005 (wizard) and E006 (list/detail/delete/status) build distinct surfaces; E006's surfaces are one management-view family for one team, not a split of one surface across epics. No work-product type mixing — design assets (E003), code/manifests (E004-E006), validation evidence (E007), and content authoring (E008) are each in their own epic. Epic sizes are consistent with an M strategy split across five implementation epics; none is multi-sprint oversized.\n58\t\n59\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n60\t\n61\tTypes are correct: E001 and E002 are genuine Investigations whose outcomes (prototype reuse basis; TP field-set decision) change what downstream epics build, and both have downstream dependents. E003-E008 are Implementations producing concrete artifacts (design spec, manifests, UI pages, validation report, published docs). E008 correctly carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. All `gated_by` targets (E003→E002, E005→E002, E006→E001) appear in the epic's direct `dependencies`, and all three gated epics populate `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach` that changes on gate failure.\n62\t\n63\tIssue (major, gating metadata — level 1 per this criterion's rubric, not a misclassification): E005 lists Investigation RHAISTRAT-8007-E001 in `dependencies`, and the summary's DAG justification explicitly calls E001→E005 a Rule 3 \"rewrite gate\", yet E005's `gated_by` names only E002. The same E001 gate is correctly recorded on E006 (`gated_by: E001`). On E005 the prototype-alignment gate exists only in `gate_failure_impact` prose (\"if the ... prototype-alignment outcome diverges ... the wizard's ... implementation basis [is] rewritten\"). `gated_by` is single-valued in the schema, so covering both gates in the field is not possible as written — but as it stands, an automated consumer detecting gates via `gated_by` will miss the E001→E005 gate. Per the criterion rubric, types are correct and this is gating-metadata incompleteness, so the criterion scores 1 rather than 0.\n64\t\n65\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n66\t\n67\tType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E003-E008 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-wiring. All eight `-ai-signals.md` rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter.\n68\t\n69\tSpot-checks hold up: E002's `human_judgment_required: -2` is justified (full-vs-curated field exposure is a PM/product decision per the strategy's open-questions owner); E001's `source_accessibility: 1` (public GitHub PR) and `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` (end-to-end confirmation needs a live cluster, peripheral) are consistent; E004's `open_questions: 1` (change fully specified; the RBAC assumption is resolved by the change itself) and E005/E006's `open_questions: 1` (gating unknowns resolved by dependencies before start) do not contradict unresolved questions; E008's `human_process_gates: -1` (editorial + mandatory technical review) matches the docs process. Computed `ai_implementability` values were not checked, per instructions.\n70\t\n71\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n72\t\n73\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs: feature-flag/backwards-compatibility ACs present on E004 (\"clusters with the flag disabled show no UI changes\"), E005 (\"wizard surface is hidden unless the feature flag is enabled\"), and E006; E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC; no replacement or konflux-chain epics exist, so those mandates do not apply. No AC states an external schedule constraint (the UXD-timeline risk is correctly placed in the summary's Health Warnings, not as an AC). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n74\t\n75\tMinor (does not reduce score): E006's scope item \"create-action entry point on the list page that opens the instance-creation wizard (navigation/routing coordinated with the wizard work)\" has no corresponding acceptance criterion — the routing coordination is prose-only. Add an AC (e.g., \"the list page's Create action navigates to the wizard route when the feature flag is enabled and the user is an admin\").\n76\t\n77\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n78\t\n79\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: the guided-form wizard, management list, detail/inspect view, delete flow with confirmation, real-time status, viewer tier, air-gapped operation, RBAC extension, feature-flag gating, UXD design, CRD/operator validation, prototype assessment, TP field-scope decision, and admin-guide documentation each map to an epic. All seven proposed strategy ACs are covered by epic ACs (wizard submission, deploying→ready status, list parity with `kubectl get`, full spec rendering, delete cleanup, viewer controls hidden, disconnected deployment). Cross-epic consistency holds: every item the upstream epics produce (field-set spec, designs, RBAC verbs, wizard route) is consumed or gated somewhere downstream, except the minor E006 entry-point AC noted above.\n80\t\n81\tStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the UXD-timeline schedule risk, the stale edit-capability question, the performance-targets question, the passive-operator remediation risk, and the RBAC merge-order coordination risk are all explicitly dispositioned in the summary's Health Warnings; the CRD-version-bump and validation-completeness risks are addressed as ACs/scope in E005 (build-time schema validation, server-side error surfacing). QE-team testing is embedded as ACs on E005/E006 rather than a separate epic — acceptable, as the strategy defines no QE deliverable beyond the flows those ACs cover. Documentation Support is listed as \"Pending human review\" (not \"Yes\"), and a docs-authoring epic exists regardless, satisfying Rule 11. No conditional branches are used; E002's bounded outcomes are correctly handled via `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` with the full-spec fallback stated.\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a73d21eb81e9941c","agent_id":"a222a03520a2c6bb0","session_id":"0ef27685-1ed8-4b23-8ac0-1ea621901635","uuid":"d137a1f9-93c1-436d-aced-f2a836a6c54a","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:57:14.207Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c6261d5fbe10067","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8007 decomposition"}
